Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.910 align:start position:0%
 
russian forces are stepping up their  attacks on towns and cities across  ukraine the strikes are hitting military  and civilian targets the assaults come  ahead of a new round of talks between  ukrainian and russian negotiators  expected to start in the next hour or so  ukrainian president vladimir zelinsky  says citizens have no choice but to keep  up their resistance despite the heavy  loss of life  a visit to boost morale ukraine's  president met wounded soldiers  recovering in a kiev military hospital  vladimir zelinski handed out medals in  recognition of their bravery  though russian forces appeared to be  widening their attacks across ukraine  zielenski vowed ultimate victory and he  indicated progress could be made as  bilateral talks continue  representatives of our countries are  holding daily talks via video  our conference has a clear task to do  everything to arrange the meeting of the  presidents  the meeting i'm sure people are waiting  for  obviously this is a difficult story  a hard path  but we need this path  on sunday moscow brought its war nearer  to the polish border  an attack on a military training ground  near lev in western ukraine was the  closest strike so far to nato territory  civilians from all over ukraine have  been fleeing to lev which felt safe  compared to eastern regions under  russian assault  now the city which is listed as a unesco  world heritage site is taking steps to  protect its cultural treasures  in an attempt to offer diversion from  the horrors of war  volunteers have organized tours around  the town for people displaced by the  fighting  i want to distract myself  from war  to think like it's fine but  i know it's not  it's it's  it's not fine right now at heart kharkiv  and i'm i'm really worried for my  friends  and my professors  if i have to judge what is happening now  in ukraine it's comparable to the  destruction of guernica of stalingrad  and with the siege of leningrad  history is repeating itself with all its  nightmares  nightmares that reached the northern  outskirts of the capital kiev on monday  morning  emergency services say a nine-story  apartment building was hit in a russian  artillery strike killing at least two  people  let's cross now to dw's nick connolly  who's standing by in the capital cave  nick new talks scheduled today is there  any reason for ukrainians to be hopeful  i fear not a great deal this is about  the optics about neither side being able  to be seen not to be open to talks  russia wants to somehow slightly reduce  the toxicity of this attack on  ukrainians show that it's open to  diplomacy but if you look at the red  lines at what russia is still trying to  achieve here in ukraine still getting  signals from russia they want ukraine to  abandon ambitions to join nato to  basically give up on its army and be  perpetually vulnerable and open to  russian intervention they want ukraine  to recognize russian ownership of annex  crimea and also to recognize those  self-proclaimed republics in the east  it's very difficult to see how president  zielenski could go back to his people  now after weeks of war and thousands of  dead and you know explain to them why he  is going along with this if ukrainians  already brought such great sacrifices  nick officials where you are in caves  say they're preparing food supplies for  the city's remaining two million  inhabitants in case of a siege what is  the situation where you are  well for now that siege situation we've  been expecting for weeks hasn't happened  yet basically people here in kiev have  been living with kind of news reports  every couple of days saying it's just  another few days until the russians  close that ring around kiev and prevent  food and backup coming in that hasn't  happened so far there is still two major  highways to the southwest and the south  that are open and you can still even  order things online  supplies medicines but even toiletries  other things online they come within a  couple of days from the west of the  country so extraordinary logistics  happening here and no sign of empty  supermarket shelves but yes the  authorities have said that they are  preparing because they do believe the  russians do want to encircle kiev and to  put that pressure on the ukrainian  government so they are prepared for two  weeks should that situation that we've  seen in mario paul repeat itself here in  kiev just on a much bigger scale  on sunday russia attacked a military  target in the far west of the country in  striking distance of nato territory how  significant an attack was that  this was important in two ways this is  about showing ukrainians that there is  no safe hinterland there is nowhere to  go that is not accessible and  destroyable by russian force these  missiles were launched from russian  planes we believe that were flying above  the black sea cruise missiles and about  35 people we now understand lost their  lives they're mainly military people but  this was just a matter of kilometers  away from the border crossings to poland  that have seen hundreds of thousands of  ukrainians queuing for kilometers in  front of those border crossing points  it's a real sense of vulnerability there  but this is also a clear signal to nato  this was a training ground where nato  troops or at least  nato member country troops from america  uk other countries were training  ukrainian forces to use those western  weapons that have proved so successful  for the ukrainians in recent weeks  so that training ground as kind of proxy  for western support for ukraine we've  heard vladimir putin recent days saying  if more significant weaponry comes in  from the west then  russia will attack those shipments those  supplies and basically we'll see any  country  aiming to help ukraine as a party to  this conflict so a very clear attempt to  try and  scare off western support for ukraine um  and a sense here that yeah this is now a  country that is a war along its  basically three thousand kilometer  border with russia and belarus  now places in the south that are now  under russian control such as the city  of herson we're seeing people fighting  back what's the situation there in those  occupied towns  we've seen some extraordinary images on  social media of people going out to  protest in spite of russian military  control of those cities in spite of  attempts to create kind of proxy states  in the country of the kind we saw in  donetsk and back in 2014 um ukrainian  politicians the local politicians not  going along with it the attempt to  create a so-called herzon people's  republic that we saw over the weekend  failing because basically the elected  ukrainian officials just wouldn't go  along with it despite a lot of pressure  being applied to them ordinary people  getting out of the streets with  ukrainian flags in some cases clambering  on russian tanks with ukrainian flags  and telling those russian soldiers to go  home we've seen the russian authorities  getting progressively more aggressive  towards those protesters firing to the  air we've heard of arrests a ukrainian  mayor institu melatuple was kidnapped by  the russian forces and they've put now  their own proxy person in charge but  definitely i think a lot more in the way  of resistance and willingness to take  risks from the ordinary kind of  residential population of those cities  than the russians certainly bargained  for well nick thank you very much for  that update dw's nick connolly in kiev  here are some other developments in the  conflict now authorities in ukraine's  besieged port city of mariupol say the  city is running out of its last reserves  of food and water they also accuse  russian forces of continuing to shell  non-military targets ukrainian president  vladimir zelinsky is urging the russian  military to allow a convoy with medical  aid and food into the city  police across russia have arrested more  than 800 people for protesting the  invasion moscow authorities say they  detained 300 people for breaching public  order police also arrested dozens in  saint petersburg as well as 30 other  cities  internet traffic monitors say the social  media platform instagram has been  restricted across russia state  regulators blocked the platform after  its owner meta said it would allow users  in ukraine to call for violence against  russian soldiers moscow had already  blocked facebook also owned by metta  a u.s journalist has been killed while  reporting near kiev brent renault was  traveling to film refugees fleeing the  town of urpin when his vehicle came  under fire another journalist was  wounded in the attack which ukraine  blames on russian  forces this footage shows the moments  after two journalists were shot in  erpine  juan arredondo was wounded and brought  to a hospital where he recounted what  had happened  we crossed one the first bridge in  europe  we're going to film other refugees  leaving  and we got into a car somebody offered  to take us to the other bridge  and we crossed the checkpoint and they  start shooting at us so the driver  turned around and they kept shooting  it's two of us my friend's friend  renault  and he's been shot and left behind  [Music]  arredondo didn't yet know that renault a  50-year-old award-winning documentary  filmmaker had died at the scene  ukrainian officials blamed russian  forces for the shooting  several media crews have come under fire  since russia's invasion of ukraine  renault is believed to be the first  foreign journalist to be killed  eopin a suburb about 20 kilometers  outside kiev has been under heavy  bombardment as russian armoured vehicles  advance towards ukraine's capital  thousands of people are fleeing in  dangerous conditions  some evacuees have come under fire but  staying is also terrifying  our building was hit with a missile and  two apartments next to ours were just  smashed  we've got cases of looting it's just  horrible very very horrible  all the windows were broken and the  balcony fell down  and the tenth floor above us was just  dust  my daughter came and said mama take your  stuff until this i didn't want to leave  my town  the refugees hoped to find relative  safety away from their hometown  rendered uninhabitable by the fighting

